<h1>Shell Sort</h1>
<p>
    Shell Sort is a variation of 
    <a href="/algorithm?id=Insertion">Insertion Sort</a>
    that has better time complexity on average.
    <br>
    <br>
    Shell Sort begins by selecting a gap.
    For this example we will say that we have an array of 100 elements, 
    and that Shell Sort chooses a gap of 50.
    Then it runs a modified version of
    <a href="/algorithm?id=Insertion">Insertion Sort</a>
    that iterates over each element of the array like normal,
    but attempts to move the element by 50 spaces when inserting it instead of moving it by one space.
    Once it finishes running this modified
    <a href="/algorithm?id=Insertion">Insertion Sort</a>
    it reduces the gap size.
    We will say that it halves the gap size, making the new gap 25 spaces.
    Then it runs the modified Insertion Sort again with the new gap size.
    It then repeats this process.
    Following the same pattern, the next gap sizes would be 12, 6, 3, and 1.
    Using a gap size of one, the modified
    <a href="/algorithm?id=Insertion">Insertion Sort</a>
    is identical to regular
    <a href="/algorithm?id=Insertion">Insertion Sort</a>,
    so after reaching a gap size of one the array is sorted.
    <br>
    <br>
    Shell Sort often uses mathematical formulas to figure out which gap sizes to use.
    These formulas are called gap sequences.
    In the above example we had an array of 100 elements, 
    and the gap sizes were 50, 25, 12, 6, 3, and 1.
    Notice how the gap size started half of of the size of the array,
    and that it shrank by half in each step.
    In this example the gap sequence would be <b>n/2</b>, with <b>n</b> being the size of the array,
    and <b>/2</b> being how the gap size is changed each iteration.
    Using another example, if the gap sizes were 25, 6, and 1, the gap sequence would be <b>n/4</b>.
    This visualization currently supports gap sequences ranging from <b>n/2</b> to <b>n-1</b>.
    <br>
    <br>
    The time complexity of Shell Sort largely depends on which gap sequence is being used.
    On average, Shell Sort has an average time complexity of <b>n<sup>1.25</sup></b> to <b>n<sup>1.5</sup></b>,
    but some gap sequences can have a time complexity of <b>n<sup>2</sup></b>.
    With optimal gap sequences, Shell Sort's best-case time complexity is <b>n logn</b>.
    <br>
    <br>
    Shell Sort was first published in 1959, and was the first sorting algortihm to reach a time complexity
    lower than <b>n<sup>2</sup></b>.
</p>

